.IX Title "At 3pm"
.TH At 3pm "2026-03-26" "perl v5.36.0" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH "NAME"
POSIX::At \- FFI bindings to the POSIX *at() family of filesystem syscalls
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 6
\&  use POSIX::At qw(
\&    AT_FDCWD AT_REMOVEDIR
\&    openat unlinkat mkdirat
\&  );
\&  use POSIX::Structs qw( POSIX::Structs::Stat );
\&  use Fcntl qw(O_RDONLY O_CREAT O_WRONLY O_DIRECTORY);
\&
\&  # Open a directory fd
\&  my $dirfd = openat(AT_FDCWD, "/some/base", O_RDONLY|O_DIRECTORY, 0);
\&
\&  # Stat a file relative to that directory
\&  my $st = POSIX::Structs::Stat\->new;
\&  fstatat($dirfd, "foo.txt", $st, 0) == 0
\&    or die "fstatat: $!";
\&  printf "mode=0%o size=%d\en", $st\->st_mode & 07777, $st\->st_size;
\&
\&  # Unlink relative to that directory
\&  unlinkat($dirfd, "old.txt", 0) == 0
\&    or die "unlinkat: $!";
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
POSIX::At provides thin \s-1FFI\s0 bindings to the modern POSIX/Linux \f(CW\*(C`*at()\*(C'\fR
family of filesystem syscalls via FFI::Platypus.
.PP
Struct arguments (e.g. for \f(CW\*(C`fstatat\*(C'\fR) use types defined in
POSIX::Structs, which must be loaded separately.  Both modules share
a single FFI::Platypus instance via \f(CW\*(C`POSIX::Structs::ffi()\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The bindings follow C semantics intentionally:
.IP "\(bu" 4
Functions return \-1 on error; check \f(CW$!\fR for the reason.
.IP "\(bu" 4
No automatic Perl magic or exception throwing.
.SH "EXPORTS"
.IX Header "EXPORTS"
By default: \f(CW\*(C`AT_FDCWD\*(C'\fR, \f(CW\*(C`AT_EMPTY_PATH\*(C'\fR.
.PP
The following may be imported by name:
.PP
.Vb 4
\&  # Constants
\&  AT_FDCWD AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW
\&  AT_EACCESS AT_REMOVEDIR AT_EMPTY_PATH
\&  RENAME_NOREPLACE RENAME_EXCHANGE RENAME_WHITEOUT
\&
\&  # Raw syscall bindings
\&  openat fstatat unlinkat mkdirat mknodat mkfifoat
\&  fchmodat fchownat utimensat linkat symlinkat readlinkat
\&  renameat renameat2 faccessat futimesat
.Ve
.PP
\&\f(CW\*(C`renameat2\*(C'\fR and \f(CW\*(C`futimesat\*(C'\fR are silently absent on platforms where the
underlying libc does not provide them.
.SH "NOTES ON utimensat AND futimesat"
.IX Header "NOTES ON utimensat AND futimesat"
These functions take a pointer to an array of two \f(CW\*(C`struct timespec\*(C'\fR (or
\&\f(CW\*(C`struct timeval\*(C'\fR) values.  The caller is responsible for allocating the
buffer.  The simplest approach is to use FFI::Platypus::Memory:
.PP
.Vb 2
\&  use FFI::Platypus::Memory qw(malloc free);
\&  use FFI::Platypus::Buffer qw(scalar_to_pointer);
\&
\&  # Pack two struct timespec values (tv_sec, tv_nsec each sint64)
\&  my $buf = pack(\*(Aqq!q!q!q!\*(Aq, $sec, 0, $sec, 0);
\&  utimensat($dirfd, "file", scalar_to_pointer($buf), 0);
.Ve
.SH "DEVELOPMENT"
.IX Header "DEVELOPMENT"
Set \f(CW\*(C`$ENV{VIM_TESTING}=1\*(C'\fR before running tests to merge \s-1STDERR\s0 into \s-1STDOUT,\s0
which makes all output appear in Vim's quickfix buffer when running \f(CW\*(C`make test\*(C'\fR
from inside the editor.
